{"question": "In a scenario where you need to optimize retrieval speed, how would you decide between fixed-size chunking and recursive splitting for a large document corpus?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "chunking", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["fixed-size chunking", "recursive splitting"], "expected_concepts": ["fixed-size vs recursive splitting", "retrieval speed optimization", "scalability considerations"], "follow_up_topics": ["retrieval latency", "document size impacts", "user experience"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Identifies fixed-size vs recursive splitting as key techniques", "Discusses retrieval speed optimization", "Mentions scalability considerations for large document corpus"], "id": "q_d631e9fc9e", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Fixed-size chunking provides predictable performance and is easier to parallelize, making it suitable for high retrieval speed.", "Recursive splitting can lead to more contextually relevant chunks but may increase retrieval time due to variable sizes.", "Consider the nature of the documents; if they have clear sections, fixed-size may be more efficient, while recursive splitting is better for complex documents."], "common_mistakes": ["Assuming one method is universally better without considering the document type.", "Neglecting the impact of indexing strategies on retrieval speed.", "Overlooking the trade-off between chunk size and context retention."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What indexing strategies would you use to enhance retrieval speed?", "How would you measure the effectiveness of each chunking method?", "Can you provide an example of a document type that benefits from recursive splitting?"]}
