{"question": "You changed chunk size from 500 to 250 tokens. How would you tell whether retrieval actually improved?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "chunking", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["retrieval metrics"], "expected_concepts": ["retrieval precision", "recall@k", "impact of chunking on user satisfaction"], "follow_up_topics": ["metric optimization", "user behavior analysis", "A/B testing"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Names retrieval precision as a primary metric", "Includes recall@k interpretation", "Connects metrics to user satisfaction in retrieval"], "id": "q_b8ceb6ca65", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Build a golden set of questions with labeled relevant chunks, and run both chunk sizes against the same set", "Compare recall@k (share of relevant chunks found in the top k) and precision@k", "Use rank-aware metrics such as MRR or nDCG to see whether the right chunk moves higher", "Check end-to-end answer quality too (faithfulness, answer correctness), since better retrieval does not always give better answers", "Look at per-query-type results, because smaller chunks may help factual lookups but hurt questions needing wider context", "a"], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing precision with recall, leading to incorrect interpretations of retrieval effectiveness.", "Neglecting to consider user satisfaction metrics alongside technical metrics.", "Focusing solely on quantitative metrics without qualitative user feedback."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What specific tools or frameworks would you use to measure these metrics?", "How would you adjust your chunking strategy based on the metrics collected?", "Can you provide an example of a situation where chunking improved retrieval performance?"]}
{"question": "After switching to semantic chunking, many retrieved chunks are tiny fragments that lack the context needed to answer. What is likely happening, and how would you fix it?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "chunking", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["semantic chunking"], "expected_concepts": ["semantic chunking issues", "contextual mismatch", "chunking optimization strategies"], "follow_up_topics": ["user experience", "data cleaning", "model retraining"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Identifies irrelevant results as a key symptom", "Mentions contextual mismatch as a likely cause", "Suggests specific chunking optimization strategies"], "id": "q_a16f2e3f81", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["The similarity breakpoint threshold is too aggressive, so the splitter cuts at almost every sentence and produces very small chunks", "Tiny chunks give noisy embeddings, and the LLM receives too little context to answer", "Fix the splitter: raise the threshold or percentile, and enforce a minimum chunk size by merging small neighbors", "Add context back at retrieval time: overlap, neighbor-chunk expansion, or parent-child (small-to-big) retrieval", "Check the chunk length distribution first, then confirm the fix with recall@k on a golden question set"], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing chunking with indexing techniques.", "Overlooking the importance of query reformulation in addressing irrelevant results."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What methods can be used to evaluate the relevance of the returned results?", "How can you optimize the embedding model used for semantic chunking?", "What metrics would you track to assess improvements after implementing fixes?"]}
