 describe a scenario where your chunking strategy might fail to maintain context?"]}
{"question": "In evaluating a retrieval system that utilizes chunk overlap, what realistic failure mode could emerge if the overlap percentage is too high, and how would you detect this?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "chunking", "difficulty": "advanced", "skills": ["chunk overlap", "retrieval evaluation", "failure mode detection"], "expected_concepts": ["chunk overlap", "retrieval redundancy", "performance metrics", "user experience impact"], "follow_up_topics": ["adjusting chunk overlap", "trade-offs in chunking strategies", "user feedback mechanisms"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Identifies chunk overlap as a key concept", "Discusses retrieval redundancy detection", "Mentions performance metrics in evaluation"], "id": "q_68a76cd5f6", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Too much overlap makes adjacent chunks near-duplicates, so the top-k fills with repeated text and crowds out other relevant chunks", "This lowers the diversity and coverage of retrieved context, which can hurt recall@k and give the LLM redundant context that wastes tokens", "It also inflates index size and embedding cost, since the same text is stored and embedded several times", "Detect it by checking top-k results for near-duplicates (high text overlap or embedding similarity) and tracking unique-source coverage per query", "Compare recall@k and answer quality at different overlap levels on a golden question set", "Fix: use modest overlap (about 10-20%), deduplicate results, or use MMR to diversify retrieval"], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing chunk overlap with chunk size, which are distinct concepts.", "Focusing solely on retrieval accuracy without considering user experience implications."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What metrics would you use to measure user engagement?", "Can you explain how the Jaccard index works in this context?", "What strategies could you implement to reduce redundancy in retrieval?"]}
{"question": "When comparing fixed-size chunking to semantic chunking under the constraint of limited computational resources, what considerations would influence your decision and which method would you choose?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "chunking", "difficulty": "advanced", "skills": ["fixed-size chunking", "semantic chunking", "resource management"], "expected_concepts": ["fixed-size vs semantic chunking", "computational resource considerations", "efficiency in retrieval"], "follow_up_topics": ["trade-offs in chunking", "impact on retrieval quality", "alternative chunking strategies"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Clearly states resource constraints affecting decision", "Compares efficiency of methods", "Mentions specific retrieval quality impacts"], "id": "q_fe248dbd9b", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "rejected", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Fixed-size chunking is simpler and requires less computational overhead, making it suitable for environments with strict resource limits.", "Semantic chunking improves retrieval accuracy by grouping related information, but it requires more processing power and memory for analysis.", "Considerations include the nature of the data, the retrieval task complexity, and the available computational resources, such as CPU and memory limits.", "If retrieval accuracy is paramount and resources allow, semantic chunking is preferred; otherwise, fixed-size chunking is more efficient."], "common_mistakes": ["Assuming fixed-size chunking is always faster without considering the context of the data.", "Neglecting the impact of data type on the choice of chunking method.", "Overlooking the trade-off between accuracy and resource usage."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What specific computational resources are you considering?", "Can you provide an example of a scenario where semantic chunking would significantly outperform fixed-size chunking?", "How would you measure the efficiency of each chunking method in practice?"]}
{"question": "If your system is designed using fixed-size chunking and you need to implement re-chunking due to document changes, what specific challenges could arise, and how would you address them?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "chunking", "difficulty": "advanced", "skills": ["re-chunking", "fixed-size chunking", "document change management"], "expected_concepts": ["re-chunking and re-indexing when documents change", "challenges in retrieval", "data consistency issues", "impact on performance"], "follow_up_topics": ["re-indexing strategies", "chunking adaptability", "performance metrics"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Identifies re-chunking challenges clearly", "Discusses data consistency issues", "Mentions impact on retrieval performance"], "id": "q_1f64b5574f", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["With fixed-size chunking an insertion or deletion shifts every later boundary, so a naive re-chunk changes all subsequent chunks and forces a full re-embed of the document", "Detect real changes by hashing each chunk's content and re-embedding only chunks whose hash changed, instead of re-processing everything", "Use stable chunk IDs (document ID plus content hash or position) and delete stale or orphaned chunks, or old text stays retrievable", "Structure-aware boundaries (headings, paragraphs) keep edits local, so a change only affects nearby chunks", "Keep document ID, version and updated-at metadata, and handle queries that arrive mid-update, for example with a versioned index and an alias swap", "Embedding cost scales with the amount of changed text, so batch updates and monitor index freshness"], "common_mistakes": ["Overlooking the impact of chunk size on retrieval accuracy.", "Failing to account for the time and resources needed for re-indexing.", "Neglecting to implement a strategy for handling concurrent updates."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What strategies can be used to minimize downtime during re-chunking?", "How would you assess the optimal chunk size for a given document type?", "Can you describe a scenario where fixed-size chunking might be preferable to dynamic chunking?"]}
{"question": "Your RAG system filters retrieval by metadata such as department and document date, but some relevant documents never show up in results. What could be going wrong, and how would you find out?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "chunking", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["metadata management", "retrieval accuracy", "failure detection"], "expected_concepts": ["metadata attached to chunks", "retrieval accuracy impacts", "failure mode detection techniques", "formatting standards"], "follow_up_topics": ["correct metadata practices", "retrieval system adjustments", "user impact assessment"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Identifies metadata formatting as a key concern", "Discusses retrieval accuracy impacts", "Mentions failure mode detection techniques"], "id": "q_4cb64db025", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Chunks with missing, null or differently named metadata fields are silently excluded by the filter, so relevant content is never retrieved", "Inconsistent value formats (dates as strings vs timestamps, \"HR\" vs \"hr\") make equality and range filters miss matches", "Over-restrictive filters, or post-filtering after the top-k search, can leave few or zero results; pre-filtering inside the vector search is safer", "Stale metadata after a document update (old version, old access level) returns outdated content or exposes content a user should not see", "Detect it by validating metadata against a schema at ingestion, measuring the percentage of chunks missing each field, and comparing result counts with and without the filter on known test queries", "Fix it by normalizing values at ingestion, setting defaults, indexing the filter fields, and alerting when a filter returns zero results"], "common_mistakes": ["Focusing solely on retrieval speed without considering accuracy.", "Overlooking the importance of schema validation and error handling in the metadata processing pipeline."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What specific metadata standards would you recommend for chunking?", "How would you implement logging for retrieval failures?", "Can you describe a scenario where incorrect metadata led to significant retrieval issues?"]}
