{"question": "In which scenarios would you prefer using embeddings with lower dimensions, and when might that choice lead to problems?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "embeddings", "difficulty": "basic", "skills": ["BERT", "Sentence Transformers", "Universal Sentence Encoder"], "expected_concepts": ["embedding dimensions and storage cost"], "follow_up_topics": ["vector normalization", "asymmetric query embeddings", "truncation of long inputs"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Mentions the trade-off between dimensions and storage cost", "Describes a problem like losing nuance or accuracy"], "id": "q_7d048d8d84", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Lower dimensional embeddings are preferred when computational efficiency and storage cost are critical, such as in mobile applications or real-time systems.", "Using lower dimensional embeddings can lead to loss of information, resulting in reduced model performance, especially in complex tasks like natural language understanding.", "Typical lower dimensions for embeddings range from 50 to 300, while higher dimensions can go up to 1000 or more, depending on the complexity of the data."], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing dimensionality reduction with feature selection, which are different processes.", "Assuming that lower dimensions always lead to better performance without considering the specific use case."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What are some techniques to determine the optimal embedding dimension?", "Can you give an example of a task where high-dimensional embeddings outperform low-dimensional ones?", "How do you evaluate the performance of embeddings in a model?"]}
{"question": "What is the difference between asymmetric query embeddings and document embeddings, and when would you use one over the other?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "embeddings", "difficulty": "basic", "skills": ["BERT", "BM25", "cross-encoders"], "expected_concepts": ["asymmetric query vs document embeddings"], "follow_up_topics": ["embedding throughput", "multilingual embeddings", "domain-specific fine-tuning"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Identifies query and document embeddings as distinct entities", "Explains a scenario where one is preferable to the other"], "id": "q_232b0b98c7", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Asymmetric query embeddings are designed to capture the intent of a user's query, while document embeddings represent the content of documents.", "Asymmetric query embeddings often use techniques like BERT or Sentence Transformers to better understand context and semantics.", "Document embeddings can be generated using methods like TF-IDF, Word2Vec, or Doc2Vec, focusing on the overall meaning of the document.", "Use asymmetric query embeddings when the goal is to match user intent with relevant documents, while document embeddings are used for clustering or categorizing documents."], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing the purpose of query embeddings with document embeddings.", "Assuming both types of embeddings are generated using the same techniques.", "Neglecting the importance of context in asymmetric query embeddings."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["Can you give an example of a scenario where asymmetric query embeddings would be preferred?", "What are some common techniques for generating document embeddings?", "How do you evaluate the effectiveness of query embeddings in a search system?"]}
{"question": "What is a common mistake beginners make when normalizing embeddings, and how can it impact the results of a retrieval system?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "embeddings", "difficulty": "basic", "skills": ["cosine similarity", "dot product", "vector normalization"], "expected_concepts": ["vector normalization, cosine vs dot product"], "follow_up_topics": ["embedding dimensions", "truncation of long inputs", "multilingual embeddings"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Mentions normalization as a crucial step", "Describes a symptom like inconsistent similarity scores"], "id": "q_f05f81e319", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Cosine similarity compares direction only; dot product also depends on vector length, so on unnormalized vectors longer vectors get higher scores regardless of meaning", "If vectors are normalized to unit length, dot product and cosine give the same ranking, so the metric must match how the vectors were produced", "Common mistake: using dot product or Euclidean distance with unnormalized embeddings, or normalizing the documents but not the query, which skews the ranking", "Many embedding models (for example OpenAI's) already return unit-normalized vectors, so check the model docs before adding extra normalization", "The similarity metric configured in the vector index (cosine, dot product, Euclidean) must match what the embedding model was trained for", "Normalization is a small preprocessing step (divide by the L2 norm), but must be applied consistently at both indexing and query time"], "common_mistakes": ["Assuming that all similarity measures yield the same results regardless of normalization.", "Not understanding the difference between cosine similarity and dot product in the context of embeddings."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What techniques can be used to normalize embeddings?", "How does the choice of similarity measure affect retrieval performance?", "Can you explain a scenario where normalization might not be necessary?"]}
