{"question": "What issues can arise from truncating long inputs when generating embeddings, and how can that affect retrieval accuracy?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "embeddings", "difficulty": "basic", "skills": ["BERT", "Sentence Transformers", "Universal Sentence Encoder"], "expected_concepts": ["truncation of long inputs"], "follow_up_topics": ["embedding dimensions", "vector normalization", "multilingual embeddings"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Names potential loss of information due to truncation", "Explains how this could lead to misleading retrieval results"], "id": "q_0a5001a262", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Embedding models have a maximum input length in tokens; text beyond it is cut off (or the call errors), so anything past the limit is not represented in the vector and cannot be found by search", "Truncation is often silent, so a document can look indexed while only its first part is searchable", "Key information near the end of a long chunk is lost, which hurts recall for queries about that content", "Fix by chunking documents to fit the model's limit (with some safety margin), counting tokens with the model's own tokenizer, and logging or alerting when inputs are truncated", "Alternatives are sliding windows with overlap, embedding sections separately, or choosing a long-context embedding model if the content really needs it"], "common_mistakes": ["Assuming that truncation has no impact on the quality of embeddings.", "Overlooking the importance of context in embeddings when discussing truncation.", "Failing to mention alternative strategies to handle long inputs."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What are some common techniques to handle long inputs in embedding generation?", "Can you explain how different embedding models handle input length?", "What metrics would you use to evaluate the impact of truncation on retrieval accuracy?"]}
