{"question": "Your vector index is getting expensive in memory and search latency as the corpus grows. How could embedding dimensionality play a part, and what would you consider changing?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "embeddings", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["embedding dimension analysis", "storage optimization", "performance tuning"], "expected_concepts": ["embedding dimensions and storage cost", "high dimension pitfalls", "performance trade-offs"], "follow_up_topics": ["storage cost management", "impact on retrieval speed", "dimensionality reduction techniques"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Identifies high storage cost as a symptom", "Discusses potential performance issues due to high dimensions", "Mentions dimensionality reduction as a solution"], "id": "q_9a533dd7c7", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Memory grows linearly with dimensions: 1 million float32 vectors at 1536 dimensions is about 6 GB, before index overhead, and higher dimensions also make search slower", "More dimensions do not always mean better retrieval; gains often flatten, and a smaller model or fewer dimensions can be nearly as good for your domain", "Options: choose a smaller embedding model, use a model that supports shortened vectors (such as the dimensions parameter on OpenAI's text-embedding-3 models), or apply PCA", "Quantization (int8 or binary) cuts memory further, usually with a small recall loss", "Always compare recall@k and answer quality on a golden question set before and after the change", "Changing dimensions means re-embedding and re-indexing everything, and queries must use the same dimension as the stored vectors"], "common_mistakes": ["Suggesting that higher dimensions always improve model performance.", "Overlooking the impact on retrieval speed and computational resources.", "Failing to mention dimensionality reduction techniques."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What specific techniques would you use to 