{"question": "How would you handle multilingual embeddings in a retrieval system designed to serve a diverse user base, and what specific challenges would you expect to face?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "embeddings", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["multilingual models", "cross-lingual retrieval", "embedding alignment"], "expected_concepts": ["multilingual embeddings", "cross-lingual semantic alignment", "handling language diversity"], "follow_up_topics": ["user experience design", "evaluation of multilingual systems", "integration with translation services"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Names language diversity as a challenge", "Discusses cross-lingual retrieval techniques", "Mentions alignment of embeddings across languages"], "id": "q_6509e8c637", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Use a retrieval-tuned multilingual embedding model (for example multilingual-e5, BGE-M3, or a commercial multilingual API) so queries and documents in different languages land in one shared vector space", "Cross-lingual retrieval lets a Hindi query match English documents, but quality is usually lower for low-resource languages and for rare domain terms", "Code-mixed text such as Hinglish, transliteration (Hindi written in Latin script) and domain jargon are common failure points, so test on real user queries", "Language-specific models or per-language indexes can beat one multilingual model for your most important languages, at the cost of more maintenance", "Chunking and tokenization behave differently across scripts, so check token limits and chunk sizes per language", "Evaluate recall@k separately for each language on a golden question set, instead of reporting one average"], "common_mistakes": ["Overlooking the importance of cultural context in multilingual embeddings.", "Assuming that a single model will perform equally well across all languages.", "Neglecting to preprocess text data for language-specific nuances."