{"question": "Explain the difference between pre-filtering and post-filtering metadata in vector search and provide an example of each.", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "vector search", "difficulty": "basic", "skills": ["metadata filtering", "vector search", "query optimization"], "expected_concepts": ["metadata filtering (pre vs post filter)", "impact on query efficiency", "use case examples"], "follow_up_topics": ["filtering strategies", "performance impacts", "real-world applications"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Defines pre-filtering and post-filtering", "Gives clear examples of each", "Explains how filtering affects search performance"], "id": "q_507ffcdc67", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Pre-filtering involves applying metadata constraints before the vector search, reducing the dataset size and improving query efficiency.", "Post-filtering applies metadata constraints after the vector search, allowing for a broader initial search but potentially increasing processing time.", "An example of pre-filtering is limiting a search to documents from a specific date range before performing the vector search.", "An example of post-filtering is retrieving the top 10 nearest neighbors and then filtering them based on a specific author or category."], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing pre-filtering with post-filtering by not recognizing the order of operations.", "Assuming that post-filtering always yields better results than pre-filtering without considering efficiency.", "Neglecting to mention the impact of dataset size on query performance."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["Can you explain how the choice between pre-filtering and post-filtering affects performance?", "What are some scenarios where post-filtering might be preferred over pre-filtering?", "How does the size of the vector database influence the effectiveness of filtering methods?"]}
{"question": "In a scenario where you have a vector search system utilizing HNSW, how would you decide on the parameters M and efSearch to optimize both recall and latency for a given use case?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "vector search", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["HNSW", "parameter tuning", "vector search optimization"], "expected_concepts": ["HNSW parameters (M, efSearch)", "recall vs latency tuning", "trade-offs between precision and recall"], "follow_up_topics": ["parameter optimization", "latency analysis", "system performance monitoring"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Names HNSW parameters (M, efSearch) as crucial factors", "Discusses trade-offs between recall and latency", "Provides a realistic use case for parameter choices"], "id": "q_33741ecd65", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["M determines the number of bi-directional links created for each node; higher values improve recall but increase memory usage and latency.", "efSearch controls the size of the dynamic list of candidate neighbors during search; increasing efSearch improves recall at the cost of latency.", "A common approach is to start with M=16 and efSearch=200, then adjust based on empirical testing for the specific dataset and use case."], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing M with efSearch; they serve different purposes in the HNSW algorithm.", "Assuming that increasing both M and efSearch will always yield better performance without considering the trade-offs."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What specific use case are you optimizing for?", "How would you measure the impact of changes to M and efSearch?", "Can you describe a scenario where high recall is more critical than low latency?"]}
{"question": "If you notice that the vector search results are lacking diversity, how would you apply MMR to improve result diversity while maintaining relevance?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "vector search", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["MMR", "result diversity", "vector ranking"], "expected_concepts": ["MMR for result diversity", "impact of diversity on user satisfaction", "balancing relevance with variety"], "follow_up_topics": ["ranking algorithms", "user engagement metrics", "evaluation of search results"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Explains the role of MMR in ranking", "Describes impact of diversity on search results", "Gives examples of relevant metrics for evaluation"], "id": "q_7494896245", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["MMR (Maximal Marginal Relevance) can be applied by adjusting the trade-off parameter, typically denoted as lambda, to balance relevance and diversity.", "A common approach is to compute the relevance score of each candidate result and then penalize results that are too similar to those already selected.", "Diversity can be quantified using similarity measures like cosine similarity, and a threshold can be set to ensure selected results are sufficiently distinct."], "common_mistakes": ["Failing to properly define the trade-off parameter, which can lead to either too much focus on diversity or too much on relevance.", "Not considering the user context or preferences when applying MMR, which can result in irrelevant diverse results."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What similarity measure would you use to calculate the diversity of results?", "How would you evaluate the effectiveness of MMR in your application?", "Can you describe a scenario where MMR might not be the best approach for improving diversity?"]}
{"question": "In a hybrid search system that uses both BM25 and dense vectors, how would you balance the contribution of each method to ensure optimal search results?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "vector search", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["BM25", "dense vector search", "reciprocal rank fusion"], "expected_concepts": ["hybrid search with BM25 and dense vectors", "reciprocal rank fusion", "weighting strategies for combining scores"], "follow_up_topics": ["search result evaluation", "comparing scoring methods", "user experience feedback"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Mentions hybrid search as a strategy", "Discusses reciprocal rank fusion as a technique", "Provides insights on weighting contributions"], "id": "q_6221210f96", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Use reciprocal rank fusion to combine BM25 and dense vector scores effectively.", "Assign weights to each method based on their performance on validation data, typically ranging from 0.5 to 0.8 for BM25 and 0.2 to 0.5 for dense vectors.", "Experiment with different weighting strategies and evaluate using metrics like NDCG or MAP to find the optimal balance."], "common_mistakes": ["Over-relying on one method without considering the strengths of the other.", "Failing to validate the chosen weights with real-world data.", "Not adjusting the weights based on the specific query types or user intent."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What metrics would you use to evaluate the effectiveness of the hybrid search?", "Can you describe a scenario where one method might outperform the other?", "How would you handle queries that are ambiguous or have multiple interpretations?"]}
{"question": "In your vector search implementation, if you need to perform real-time updates and deletes, what considerations would you have to account for in the indexing strategy?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "vector search", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["index management", "real-time updates", "vector search performance"], "expected_concepts": ["index updates and deletes", "impact of updates on search accuracy", "efficiency of indexing strategies"], "follow_up_topics": ["performance impacts of indexing", "different indexing techniques", "data consistency"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Identifies challenges with real-time updates", "Discusses indexing strategies relevant to performance", "Mentions potential impacts on search accuracy"], "id": "q_8da5599113", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Choose an indexing strategy that supports incremental updates, such as using a hybrid index that combines both batch and real-time updates.", "Implement a mechanism to handle versioning of vectors to ensure that updates do not affect the accuracy of search results.", "Consider the trade-off between indexing speed and search performance, potentially using a separate index for real-time updates to minimize latency."], "common_mistakes": ["Neglecting to account for the impact of deleted vectors on search accuracy.", "Assuming that all indexing strategies can handle real-time updates without performance degradation."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What specific indexing techniques would you use for real-time updates?", "How would you measure the impact of updates on search accuracy?", "Can you explain how versioning of vectors works in your implementation?"]}
{"question": "Your vector search p95 latency has grown from 80 ms to 400 ms as the collection grew. Where would you look first, and what would you adjust?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "vector search", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["latency measurement", "performance monitoring", "vector search optimization"], "expected_concepts": ["recall vs latency tuning", "important latency metrics to track", "interpretation of latency data"], "follow_up_topics": ["performance bottlenecks", "system resource utilization", "improving response times"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Names specific latency metrics to track", "Discusses interpretation of metric trends", "Connects metrics to potential system issues"], "id": "q_7021aab901", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "approved", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Track tail latency (p95 or p99) instead of the average, and break the total time into its parts: query embedding call, vector search, filtering, reranking and network", "Check whether the index still fits in memory; once it spills to disk, latency jumps sharply as vectors and dimensions grow", "Look at the ANN search settings: a higher efSearch (or numCandidates in Atlas) improves recall but costs latency, so tune it against recall@k on a golden set instead of guessing", "Check filters: a restrictive metadata filter applied after the vector search, or on unindexed fields, can be slow and return few results; filter fields should be declared in the index", "Reduce cost with fewer dimensions, quantization, or a smaller top-k, and make sure the index is built (not falling back to exact search)", "Consider caching repeated queries and sharding or scaling the cluster only after the checks above point to a capacity limit"], "common_mistakes": ["Focusing only on query response time without considering other metrics.", "Neglecting to analyze the impact of data size on latency.", "Assuming that increasing hardware resources will automatically reduce latency."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What tools would you use to monitor these metrics?", "How would you prioritize which metrics to address first?", "Can you explain how caching can improve vector search performance?"]}
{"question": "In a scenario where you are using both exact and approximate nearest neighbor search methods, what criteria would you use to decide which method to employ for specific queries?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "vector search", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["exact nearest neighbor search", "approximate nearest neighbor search", "query optimization"], "expected_concepts": ["exact (flat) vs approximate nearest neighbor search", "criteria for choosing search methods", "trade-offs between accuracy and speed"], "follow_up_topics": ["query performance metrics", "user experience considerations", "method comparison studies"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Describes trade-offs between exact and approximate methods", "Mentions criteria for choosing based on query type", "Provides examples of query scenarios for each method"], "id": "q_397ab69514", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Consider the size of the dataset; exact methods are preferable for smaller datasets (e.g., <10,000 vectors).", "Evaluate the required accuracy; use exact methods when precision is critical (e.g., in medical or legal applications).", "Assess the query speed requirements; approximate methods are suitable for real-time applications where speed is prioritized over accuracy.", "Analyze the dimensionality of the data; approximate methods often perform better in high-dimensional spaces due to the curse of dimensionality.", "Consider the computational resources available; approximate methods can reduce memory and processing time significantly."], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing the definitions of exact and approximate methods.", "Overlooking the impact of dimensionality on search performance.", "Assuming that approximate methods always sacrifice too much accuracy."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What specific approximate nearest neighbor algorithms are you familiar with?", "Can you provide an example of a scenario where you would prefer an approximate method?", "How do you measure the accuracy of your search results?"]}
