{"question": "How can context stuffing lead to hallucination in a RAG system, and what strategies would you implement to mitigate this issue?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "hallucination", "difficulty": "intermediate", "skills": ["context management", "prompt optimization", "data handling"], "expected_concepts": ["context stuffing and lost-in-the-middle", "prompt length management", "input filtering techniques"], "follow_up_topics": ["contextual relevance", "user comprehension issues", "data preprocessing methods"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Defines context stuffing and lost-in-the-middle accurately", "Suggests methods for managing prompt length", "Mentions input filtering techniques as mitigation strategies"], "id": "q_81a3f73326", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Context stuffing occurs when too much information is fed into the model, leading to confusion and irrelevant outputs.", "Hallucination in RAG systems can be mitigated by implementing input filtering techniques to prioritize relevant context.", "Strategies include using a sliding window approach to manage prompt length and ensuring that only the most pertinent information is included.", "Regularly updating the retrieval mechanism to improve the relevance of retrieved documents can also help reduce hallucination.", "Monitoring output quality and adjusting the context length dynamically based on model performance can enhance accuracy."], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing context stuffing with general overfitting of the model.", "Suggesting that increasing the model size alone can solve hallucination issues without addressing context management.", "Overlooking the importance of retrieval quality in the RAG framework."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What specific input filtering techniques would you recommend?", "Can you explain the sliding window approach in more detail?", "How would you measure the effectiveness of your mitigation strategies?"]}
