{"question": "What is Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR), and how can it be used to evaluate the effectiveness of a retrieval system?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "retrieval evaluation", "difficulty": "basic", "skills": ["Mean Reciprocal Rank"], "expected_concepts": ["MRR", "definition and calculation method", "interpretation of MRR values"], "follow_up_topics": ["limitations of MRR", "comparison with other metrics", "practical applications of MRR"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Defines MRR accurately", "Describes how to calculate it", "Explains the significance of MRR values"], "id": "q_bb416d7dd4", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) is a statistical measure used to evaluate the effectiveness of retrieval systems by calculating the average of the reciprocal ranks of the first relevant document.", "MRR is calculated as MRR = (1/Q) * Σ(1/rank_i) for Q queries, where rank_i is the rank of the first relevant document for the i-th query.", "MRR values range from 0 to 1, where a value of 1 indicates that all queries returned the relevant document at the top rank, and lower values indicate poorer performance."], "common_mistakes": ["Confusing MRR with other metrics like Precision or Recall.", "Not explaining how to calculate MRR or what the components of the formula represent."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["Can you provide an example of calculating MRR for a set of queries?", "How does MRR compare to other evaluation metrics like NDCG?", "What are the limitations of using MRR for evaluating retrieval systems?"]}
{"question": "What is the difference between human relevance labeling and using LLMs for labeling in retrieval evaluation, and what common mistake might occur if LLMs are solely relied upon?", "topic": "RAG", "subtopic": "retrieval evaluation", "difficulty": "basic", "skills": ["human relevance labeling", "LLM relevance labeling"], "expected_concepts": ["relevance labeling: human vs LLM judge", "bias and accuracy in labeling", "when to use each type"], "follow_up_topics": ["improving label accuracy", "impact of bias on results", "evaluation of labeling methods"], "evaluation_criteria": ["Describes differences between human and LLM labeling", "Identifies a common mistake with LLMs", "Discusses accuracy and bias issues"], "id": "q_b69285eed0", "source": "llm_generated", "review_status": "pending", "reference_answer": {"key_points": ["Human relevance labeling involves subjective judgment based on context and user intent, while LLMs use statistical patterns from training data to assess relevance.", "LLMs may introduce biases based on their training data, potentially leading to inaccurate relevance assessments.", "Human evaluators can consider nuanced factors such as user satisfaction and specific query context, which LLMs may overlook."], "common_mistakes": ["Assuming LLMs can fully replace human evaluators without loss of accuracy.", "Neglecting the importance of context in relevance assessments when using LLMs."]}, "follow_up_questions": ["What are some scenarios where human labeling is preferred over LLMs?", "How can we mitigate bias when using LLMs for relevance labeling?", "What metrics can be used to evaluate the accuracy of LLM-generated relevance labels?"]}
